Usage: date [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Display the current time in the given FORMAT, or set the system date.

FORMAT controls the output. Interpreted sequences are:
  %Y   Year (4 digits)
  %y   Year (2 digits)
  %m   Month (01-12)
  %d   Day (01-31)
  %H   Hour (00-23)
  %I   Hour (01-12)
  %M   Minute (00-59)
  %S   Second (00-60)
  %F   Full date; same as %Y-%m-%d
  %T   Time; same as %H:%M:%S
  %z   Numeric time zone offset
  %p   AM/PM
  %a   Abbreviated weekday name
  %A   Full weekday name
  %b   Abbreviated month name
  %B   Full month name

OPTIONS:
  -d, --date ARG        display time described by STRING, not 'now'
  -u, --utc             Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)
  -R, --rfc-email       output RFC 5322 compliant date string
      --rfc-2822        output RFC 2822 compliant date string
  -I, --iso-8601[=ARG]  output ISO 8601 date/time
      --rfc-3339 ARG    output RFC 3339 date/time
  -r, --reference ARG   display the last modification time of FILE
  -f, --file ARG        display date strings from DATEFILE, one per line
      --universal       alias for --utc
      --debug           annotate the parsed date, and warn about dubious usage
                        to stderr
      --resolution      output the available resolution of timestamps
      --set ARG         set time described by STRING
  -s ARG                set time described by STRING
      --help            display this help and exit
  -V, --version         output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
